WEBVTT

00:00.630 --> 00:02.700
Is the system of
linear equations below

00:02.700 --> 00:05.370
consistent or inconsistent?

00:05.370 --> 00:08.330
And they give us x
plus 2y is equal to 13

00:08.330 --> 00:11.590
and 3x minus y is
equal to negative 11.

00:11.590 --> 00:13.205
So to answer this
question, we need

00:13.205 --> 00:16.430
to know what it means to be
consistent or inconsistent.

00:16.430 --> 00:18.460
So a consistent
system of equations.

00:20.980 --> 00:24.815
has at least one solution.

00:28.810 --> 00:31.510
And an inconsistent system of
equations, as you can imagine,

00:31.510 --> 00:32.685
has no solutions.

00:36.370 --> 00:38.300
So if we think about
it graphically,

00:38.300 --> 00:43.600
what would the graph of a
consistent system look like?

00:43.600 --> 00:45.620
Let me just draw a
really rough graph.

00:45.620 --> 00:50.750
So that's my x-axis,
and that is my y-axis.

00:50.750 --> 00:53.480
So if I have just two
different lines that intersect,

00:53.480 --> 00:55.250
that would be consistent.

00:55.250 --> 00:58.860
So that's one line, and
then that's another line.

00:58.860 --> 01:01.300
They clearly have
that one solution

01:01.300 --> 01:03.300
where they both
intersect, so that

01:03.300 --> 01:04.980
would be a consistent system.

01:04.980 --> 01:07.179
Another consistent
system would be

01:07.179 --> 01:08.970
if they're the same
line, because then they

01:08.970 --> 01:12.430
would intersect at
a ton of points,

01:12.430 --> 01:14.250
actually at an infinite
number of points.

01:14.250 --> 01:16.520
So let's say one of the
lines looks like that.

01:16.520 --> 01:19.190
And then the other line is
actually the exact same line.

01:19.190 --> 01:21.200
So it's exactly
right on top of it.

01:21.200 --> 01:24.220
So those two intersect at
every point along those lines,

01:24.220 --> 01:26.500
so that also would
be consistent.

01:26.500 --> 01:30.010
An inconsistent system
would have no solutions.

01:30.010 --> 01:34.230
So let me again draw my axes.

01:34.230 --> 01:36.940
Let me once again draw my axes.

01:36.940 --> 01:38.470
It will have no solutions.

01:38.470 --> 01:40.300
And so the only way
that you're going

01:40.300 --> 01:43.030
to have two lines
in two dimensions

01:43.030 --> 01:46.280
have no solutions is if
they don't intersect,

01:46.280 --> 01:47.990
or if they are parallel.

01:47.990 --> 01:50.580
So one line could
look like this.

01:50.580 --> 01:52.802
And then the other line
would have the same slope,

01:52.802 --> 01:54.010
but it would be shifted over.

01:54.010 --> 01:56.056
It would have a
different y-intercept,

01:56.056 --> 01:57.180
so it would look like this.

01:57.180 --> 02:00.900
So that's what an inconsistent
system would look like.

02:00.900 --> 02:02.720
You have parallel lines.

02:02.720 --> 02:05.650
This right here is inconsistent.

02:05.650 --> 02:07.610
So what we could do is
just do a rough graph

02:07.610 --> 02:11.250
of both of these lines
and see if they intersect.

02:11.250 --> 02:13.620
Another way to do it is,
you could look at the slope.

02:13.620 --> 02:16.700
And if they have the same slope
and different y-intercepts,

02:16.700 --> 02:18.610
then you'd also have
an inconsistent system.

02:18.610 --> 02:20.410
But let's just graph them.

02:20.410 --> 02:27.506
So let me draw my x-axis
and let me draw my y-axis.

02:30.350 --> 02:34.389
So this is x and then this is y.

02:34.389 --> 02:36.430
And then there's a couple
of ways we could do it.

02:36.430 --> 02:38.346
The easiest way is really
just find two points

02:38.346 --> 02:41.730
on each of these that satisfy
each of these equations,

02:41.730 --> 02:43.590
and that's enough
to define a line.

02:43.590 --> 02:47.230
So for this first one, let's
just make a little table

02:47.230 --> 02:48.890
of x's and y's.

02:48.890 --> 02:56.940
When x is 0, you have
2y is equal to 13,

02:56.940 --> 03:05.170
or y is equal to 13/2, which
is the same thing as 6 and 1/2.

03:05.170 --> 03:08.710
So when x is 0, y is 6 and 1/2.

03:08.710 --> 03:10.390
I'll just put it
right over here.

03:10.390 --> 03:14.440
So this is 0 comma 13/2.

03:14.440 --> 03:16.890
And then let's just see
what happens when y is 0.

03:16.890 --> 03:19.950
When y is 0, then
2 times y is 0.

03:19.950 --> 03:22.230
You have x equaling 13.

03:22.230 --> 03:24.150
x equals 13.

03:24.150 --> 03:26.810
So we have the point 13 comma 0.

03:26.810 --> 03:29.920
So this is 0, 6 and
1/2, so 13 comma 0

03:29.920 --> 03:31.420
would be right about there.

03:31.420 --> 03:34.780
We're just trying to
approximate-- 13 comma 0.

03:34.780 --> 03:37.750
And so this line right
up here, this equation

03:37.750 --> 03:39.480
can be represented by this line.

03:39.480 --> 03:41.880
Let me try my best to draw it.

03:41.880 --> 03:45.050
It would look
something like that.

03:45.050 --> 03:47.950
Now let's worry about this one.

03:47.950 --> 03:49.400
Let's worry about that one.

03:49.400 --> 03:51.980
So once again, let's make a
little table, x's and y's.

03:51.980 --> 03:54.840
I'm really just looking for
two points on this graph.

03:54.840 --> 03:59.970
So when x is equal to
0, 3 times 0 is just 0.

03:59.970 --> 04:02.460
So you get negative y
is equal to negative 11,

04:02.460 --> 04:04.890
or you get y is equal to 11.

04:04.890 --> 04:08.380
So you have the point 0, 11, so
that's maybe right over there.

04:08.380 --> 04:11.110
0 comma 11 is on that line.

04:11.110 --> 04:16.680
And then when y is 0,
you have 3x minus 0

04:16.680 --> 04:20.640
is equal to negative 11, or
3x is equal to negative 11.

04:20.640 --> 04:22.250
Or if you divide
both sides by 3,

04:22.250 --> 04:24.060
you get x is equal
to negative 11/3.

04:28.220 --> 04:33.320
And this is the exact same
thing as negative 3 and 2/3.

04:33.320 --> 04:40.490
So when y is 0, you have x
being negative 3 and 2/3.

04:40.490 --> 04:43.600
So maybe this is about
6, so negative 3 and 2/3

04:43.600 --> 04:46.090
would be right about here.

04:46.090 --> 04:51.440
So this is the point
negative 11/3 comma 0.

04:51.440 --> 04:55.380
And so the second equation will
look like something like this.

04:55.380 --> 04:57.790
Will look something like that.

04:57.790 --> 05:01.270
Now clearly-- and I might have
not been completely precise

05:01.270 --> 05:04.230
when I did this hand-drawn
graph-- clearly these two

05:04.230 --> 05:05.790
guys intersect.

05:05.790 --> 05:07.360
They intersect right over here.

05:07.360 --> 05:08.734
And to answer
their question, you

05:08.734 --> 05:11.380
don't even have to find the
point that they intersect at.

05:11.380 --> 05:13.060
We just have to
see, very clearly,

05:13.060 --> 05:14.950
that these two lines intersect.

05:14.950 --> 05:18.480
So this is a consistent
system of equations.

05:18.480 --> 05:20.510
It has one solution.

05:20.510 --> 05:23.840
You just have to have at least
one in order to be consistent.

05:23.840 --> 05:27.567
So once again, consistent
system of equations.
